CSE 550 Problem Set 2 — Bufferbloat
====================================

Instructions to reproduce results
----------------------------------

Prerequisites
  - Mininet installed (tested on Ubuntu 20.04 / Linux 5.4)
  - Python 3.8+
  - iperf (v2), wget, ping  (all present on the Mininet VM image)
  - matplotlib (installed automatically by run.sh if missing)

Run the full experiment (both queue sizes, all plots):

    sudo ./run.sh

The script runs two 200-second Mininet experiments (q=20 and q=100) and
produces the six required plots in the top-level directory:

    buffer-q20.png   cwnd-q20.png   rtt-q20.png
    buffer-q100.png  cwnd-q100.png  rtt-q100.png

Raw data land in bb-q20/ and bb-q100/ respectively.

Implementation notes
--------------------
The skeleton code assumed Python 2 and the tcp_probe kernel module, both of
which are unavailable on this kernel (5.4.0-42-generic).  The following
changes were made:

  1. All scripts ported to Python 3 (xreadlines->iterate, map()->list(map()),
     izip_longest->zip_longest, bytes.decode() for subprocess output, etc.)

  2. http/webserver.py ported from SimpleHTTPServer/SocketServer to
     http.server/socketserver.

  3. tcp_probe replaced by polling "ss -i dst :5001" inside h1's network
     namespace every 100 ms (start_cwnd_monitor in bufferbloat.py).
     Output format: unix_timestamp,cwnd_in_segments.
     plot_tcpprobe.py was rewritten to parse this format.

  4. curl replaced by wget (curl binary not installed on the VM image).

Topology
--------
  h1 -- (1 Gb/s, 10 ms) -- s0 -- (1.5 Mb/s, 10 ms, maxq pkts) -- h2

  Two 10 ms netem links -> one-way propagation = 20 ms -> minimum RTT ~= 40 ms.

Fetch time results
------------------
  Queue size 100:  avg = 6.30 s,  std = 2.44 s  (27 samples)
  Queue size  20:  avg = 2.76 s,  std = 1.00 s  (48 samples)


Answers to questions
--------------------

Q1. Why do you see a difference in webpage fetch times with short and large
    router buffers?

    With a large buffer (q=100) the long-lived iperf flow fills the buffer
    completely before TCP backs off.  A small web-page fetch must wait behind
    ~100 packets x 8 ms/packet ~= 800 ms of additional queuing delay for every
    round-trip, stretching fetch times to 6-10 s on average.  With a small
    buffer (q=20) the queue holds at most ~160 ms of extra delay, so short
    flows see much lower queuing time (avg ~= 2.8 s) and the long-lived flow
    is forced to back off more frequently, keeping latency manageable.

Q2. What is the transmit queue length on the NIC reported by ifconfig?
    For a 100 Mb/s drain rate, what is the maximum wait time?

    "ifconfig eth0" reports:  txqueuelen 1000

    Maximum wait time at 100 Mb/s:
        1000 packets x 1500 bytes/packet x 8 bits/byte
        ----------------------------------------------- = 0.12 s = 120 ms
                  100,000,000 bits/s

Q3. How does the RTT reported by ping vary with queue size?
    Give a symbolic equation.

    Each packet queued at the bottleneck (rate C, MTU bytes) adds MTU*8/C
    seconds of additional delay.  With q packets in the queue:

        RTT(q) = RTT_prop + q * (MTU * 8) / C

    where
        RTT_prop  = minimum (propagation) RTT  ~= 40 ms in our setup
        MTU       = 1500 bytes
        C         = 1.5 Mb/s = 1,500,000 b/s
        q         = current queue occupancy (packets)

    So each additional queued packet adds ~8 ms of delay.

    Expected maxima:
        q=100 -> RTT_max ~= 40 + 100*8 = 840 ms
        q=20  -> RTT_max ~= 40 +  20*8 = 200 ms

    Observed maxima were roughly double (~1500 ms and ~417 ms), consistent
    with TCLink applying maxq to both ends of the slow link (s0-eth2 and
    h2-eth0), effectively doubling the bottleneck buffer.

Q4. Identify and describe two ways to mitigate the bufferbloat problem.

    1. Active Queue Management (AQM) -- algorithms such as CoDel (Controlled
       Delay) or FQ-CoDel measure actual sojourn time of packets in the queue
       and drop/ECN-mark packets early when delay exceeds a target (e.g. 5 ms).
       This keeps the buffer shallow without requiring smaller static limits,
       preserving throughput while bounding latency.

    2. Smaller static buffer sizes -- setting queue limits close to the
       bandwidth-delay product (BDP = C * RTT_prop ~= 7.5 kB ~= 5 packets at
       1.5 Mb/s / 40 ms) prevents the buffer from accumulating more data than
       the network can absorb in one RTT.  Short flows (web pages) experience
       much lower latency; the trade-off is more frequent drops for long-lived
       flows, which causes more TCP retransmissions.
